; i2s_tx.loom
;
; Decisions (2026-11-03): underrun is per word (a missing word goes out as
; zero), so a host that misses only a left word shifts later words one
; channel; a per-frame rule did not fit. One thread reaches 16-bit stereo
; at 65.1 kHz (SCK 2.083 MHz at 50 MHz); 96 kHz and 24-in-32 at 48 kHz are
; out of reach, and at 48 kHz the host must refill INQ within about 40 us.
;
; I2S controller (master) transmitter: SCK, WS and SD out, 16-bit stereo
; samples from the host through INQ[0]. Written on 2026-11-03, after the
; hardware was complete (D-032), as a protocol Loom was not designed for
; (D-035). It uses the bit engine's manual shifter (SHO, built for the
; serial protocols: SPI, UART, CAN), MSB first, for SD; the deadline-latched
; pin write (SETP pin, v, D, built for clock-exact UART and WS2812 edges)
; for every SCK edge; and the FIFOs. What no hardware does: the continuous,
; gap-free bit clock, the word-select timing and the frame layout are all
; firmware. An I2S peripheral is a shift register clocked by a divider;
; Loom spends one thread's every slot on it (see "Limits" below).
;
; Specification: NXP UM11732 "I2S bus specification" Rev 3.0, 17 Feb 2022
; (the Philips text of Feb 1986, revised 5 June 1996):
;   3.1  two's complement, MSB first; the receiver latches SD on the
;        leading (rising) SCK edge; data may change on either edge.
;   3.2  WS = 0 left, 1 right, latched on the rising edge; WS changes one
;        SCK period before the MSB.
;   Table 3: controller clock tHC, tLC >= 0.35T with a fixed mark/space
;        ratio (note 2); transmitter delay tdtr <= 0.8T and hold
;        thtr >= 0, both from the RISING edge (note 5); receiver set-up
;        tsr >= 0.2T, hold thr >= 0.
;
; Pins: SCK = OUT0, SD = OUT1, WS = OUT2 (move with --pin; BIDIR pins work,
; the program drives their output enables).
;
; Host format (FIFO space, thread 0): one 16-bit two's complement sample
; per word, left then right, left first. 32 SCK per frame (16 per channel),
; so fs = SCK / 32.
;
; Rate: one tick is half an SCK period, SCK = clk / (2 * tick). The host
; sets TICK_INT and TICK_FRAC (debug space) before RUN. The schedule is
; proved for a tick of at least 12 clocks (.tick 12): SCK <= 2.083 MHz,
; fs <= 65.1 kHz at 50 MHz.
;     48 kHz:   TICK_INT 16, TICK_FRAC 71  (16.277 clocks; fs 47.998 kHz)
;     44.1 kHz: TICK_INT 17, TICK_FRAC 183 (17.715 clocks; fs 44.100 kHz)
; Each SCK edge is a staged write that lands on its deadline. With a tick
; of 17 clocks or more every edge is on its exact tick (within one clock
; of the ideal fractional time; 44.1 kHz). Below that the WAITD of an
; interval can issue in the tick's own X cycle when the WAITD before it
; completed 3 clocks late, and the edge then lands at that WAITD's commit,
; one clock after its tick (SEMANTICS 6.10 rule 2): at 48 kHz an edge is
; within 2 clocks (40 ns) of the ideal and the mean rate is exact. At 12
; clocks the interval has no slack and the edges fall on the slot grid,
; which with 12 = 3 slots is exactly periodic (SEMANTICS 2).
;
; Bit timing. Each bit is a rise half and a fall half of one tick each:
;     rise: WAITD (SCK rises) ; SHO (SD = the next bit) ; SETP SCK, 0, D
;     fall: WAITD (SCK falls) ; <one op>                ; SETP SCK, 1, D
; SD is clocked out on the leading edge (3.1 allows either edge): it
; changes one slot after SCK rises: measured 4 clocks later at tick 12
; and 6 to 9 at 48 and 44.1 kHz (WS the same), so the hold thtr is at
; least 4 clocks (80 ns), the delay tdtr at most 9/32.6 = 0.28T against
; 0.8T, and the receiver's set-up the rest of the period, 0.72T or more.
; (An earlier version changed SD after the falling edge; at 48 kHz its
; delay reached 26 clocks against 0.8T = 26.04, inside Table 3 by a
; hair. This one has margin everywhere.)
;
; The LSB and WS. WS must change one SCK before the MSB, i.e. together
; with the LSB, after the rising edge of bit 15. The rise half has one
; slot, so the LSB does not come from SHO: one OUT writes SD and WS
; together from r6 = LSB of the word | the next word's WS, computed in
; earlier op slots. SD and WS form the OUT group, so they must be adjacent
; pins (or SCK must not lie between them).
;
; Word work. The op slot of a fall half is the loop's DJNZ for bits 1..4.
; Bits 5..16 are unrolled, and their op slots do the per-word work, one
; each, in this order:
;     b5  MOV r6, r0       the word being sent (still in r0) ...
;     b6  ANDI r6, 1       ... its LSB ...
;     b7  SHLI r6, BSD     ... in SD's place in the OUT word
;     b8  XORI r2, MWS     the next word's WS level
;     b9  OR r6, r6, r2    LSB and new WS: the OUT word for bit 15's rise
;     b10 LDI r0, 0        underrun rule: the next word is 0 unless ...
;     b11 WAITB INQ_NE, T  ... the host has queued one (a test, not a wait:
;     b12 BT under          it follows a completed WAITD, so TD is reached)
;     b13 POP r0           declared bounded (the WAITB argument of ws2812)
;     b14 LDI r3, NLOOP    loop count for the next word
;     (b15's rise half: OUT r6, the LSB and the new WS together)
;     b15 LDSR r0          the next word into SR
;     (b16's rise half: SHO, the next word's MSB)
;     b16 JMP bits
; Underrun rule: a word the host has not queued by bit 11 of the word
; before it is sent as 0 (silence), and the frame timing does not move.
; The `under` path replaces b13 and rejoins at b14 with the same slots.
;
; Start: SCK idles low; the program waits for the first queued word, then
; sends a preamble of 12 SCK periods with WS = 1 and SD = 0 (the tail of
; an empty right word, entered at b5), so the first left word starts with
; a correct WS change. From then on SCK never stops.
;
; Limits (stated plainly). SCK <= 2.083 MHz at 50 MHz: 16-bit stereo up
; to 65 kHz, which covers 44.1 and 48 kHz; 96 kHz, and 24-bit samples in
; 32-bit slots at 48 kHz (both 3.072 MHz), are out of reach for one
; thread. 48 kHz stereo is 96 k words/s through the host port with a
; 4-word INQ, 41.7 us of audio: the host, not the chip, is the hard part.
; SD and WS move on the 4-clock slot grid; only SCK is clock-exact.
; No MCLK is generated: the receiver must derive its clock from SCK (for
; example a PCM5102A in its 3-wire mode).
;
; Registers: r0 the word being sent, then the next one; r2 the WS level of
; the word being sent, in WS's place in the OUT word; r3 the loop count;
; r6 the OUT word for the LSB; r7 the .csr scratch.

        .imem   512
        .thread 0
        .pins   SCK = OUT0, SD = OUT1, WS = OUT2
        .tick   12                      ; half an SCK period, at the fastest
        .equ    NLOOP = 4               ; bits 1..4 run in the loop
        .equ    OUTB = min(pin(SD), pin(WS))
        .equ    OUTN = max(pin(SD), pin(WS)) - OUTB + 1
        .equ    BSD = pin(SD) - OUTB    ; SD's bit in an OUT word
        .equ    MWS = 1 << (pin(WS) - OUTB)   ; WS's bit in an OUT word

start:  .csr    OUTGRP, OUTB | (OUTN << 5)  ; OUT writes SD and WS
        .csr    BE_PINS, pin(SD)        ; SHO drives SD
        .csr    BE_CFG, 2               ; DIR = 1: MSB first, NRZ, no CRC
        SETP    SCK, 0                  ; SCK idles low
        SETP    SD, 0
        SETP    WS, 1                   ; WS = 1: as if a right word ended
        OEP     SCK, 1                  ; no-ops on OUT pins, needed on
        OEP     WS, 1                   ; BIDIR pins
        OEP     SD, 1
        LDI     r2, MWS                 ; the preamble's WS level
        LDI     r0, 0                   ; the preamble's word: zeros
        LDSR    r0
        WAITB   INQ_NE                  ; the first sample is queued
        SETD    1                       ; re-anchor after the unbounded wait
        JMP     b5                      ; the preamble: b5..b16, WS = 1

; ------------------------------------------------- bits 1..4 of a word
bits:   SETP    SCK, 1, D
        WAITD   1                       ; SCK rises: the receiver latches
        SHO                             ; SD = the next bit
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1                       ; SCK falls
        DJNZ    r3, bits

; ------------------------------------------------- bits 5..16, unrolled
b5:     SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b5
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        MOV     r6, r0                          ; the word being sent

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b6
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        ANDI    r6, 1                           ; its LSB

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b7
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        SHLI    r6, BSD                         ; in SD's place

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b8
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        XORI    r2, MWS                         ; the next word's WS level

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b9
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        OR      r6, r6, r2                      ; LSB | new WS: bit 15's OUT word

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b10
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        LDI     r0, 0                           ; underrun: the next word is 0

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b11
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T                       ; a test: TD is reached, so T = 1 at once if INQ is empty

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b12
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        BT      under                           ; nothing queued: send 0

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b13
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        .bounded "the WAITB INQ_NE, T of bit 11 completed with T = 0 (the BT of bit 12 fell through; nothing between them writes T), so INQ held a word in its X cycle; only this thread pops INQ (SEMANTICS 6.7)"
        POP     r0                              ; one slot: it cannot stall

b14:    SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b14
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        LDI     r3, NLOOP

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b15
        WAITD   1
        OUT     r6                              ; the LSB and the new WS together
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        LDSR    r0                              ; the next word, after the LSB went out

        SETP    SCK, 1, D                       ; b16
        WAITD   1
        SHO                                     ; the next word's MSB
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        JMP     bits

; ------------------------------------------------- b13 on an underrun
under:  SETP    SCK, 1, D
        WAITD   1
        SHO
        SETP    SCK, 0, D
        WAITD   1
        JMP     b14                     ; r0 stays 0: the word is silence
